From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: damnbitimcrit <333091414+damnbitimcrit@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [GPU] Keep memory export results over CPU writes to their
 pages

Unless readback_memexport is on, what shaders write with memory export
exists only in the host GPU copy of guest memory. A CPU write anywhere
in one of its 4 KB pages invalidates the page, and the next request
uploads the whole page again from guest memory, which never got the
results. Halo 3 simulates its particles, contrails, light volumes and
beams on the GPU this way, in storages that share their first and last
pages with CPU data it writes every frame (the in-use bitmaps of its
datum arrays): the stretched plasma trails and drop-pod smoke.
readback_memexport fixes it by waiting for the GPU at every export.

With memexport_keep_gpu_data (default off, read at each use, so per-game
configuration works), the shared memory remembers the byte ranges
written by memory export inside windows that title code registers as
GPU-only (memexport_keep.h), and uploads skip those bytes, leaving them
as the GPU wrote them; the other bytes of the page still come from guest
memory, and so do whole export targets outside the windows. A free,
decommit, protection change or watched file read of the pages drops the
kept bytes (Halo 3 protects its game state that way before every load,
so loaded saves and films take their own data, as before).

Halo 3 registers its four effect storages (headers set up by
sub_8245F1A8: stride, count, address) from the main loop hook once they
exist. Not every export target is GPU-only: the CPU fills the ripple
staging buffers the GPU exported to the frame before (sub_8244AE08).

Counters for titles that log statistics: memory export ranges, GPU-
written pages a CPU write invalidated, and bytes kept on upload. Each
distinct export range is logged once (up to 64).
